< Ad platforms and marketplaces show bidders a bid landscape, the estimated chance of winning at each candidate bid, and leave the choice to them or to their agents, which increasingly run on reasoning language models. We ask where the value of the reasoning tokens goes in that decision. Six small ($\le$14B) open models choose among 5 or 7 bids with empirical win curves from eBay prices, under forced budgets of up to 768 tokens, and are scored on held-out auctions against an exact calculator. They recover 42--85\% of the calculator's lead, and arithmetic is a large part of the remaining loss for one model only. The rest has two sources. For the Qwen models, 74--79\% of it comes from the budget: the list of options is unfinished when the answer is due. Phi-mini and Granite-8B finish the list and then answer against it: even without forcing, 40--50\% of their finished traces end on a bid other than the one the trace ranked best, in a way that follows list position within a scenario and replicates on synthetic auctions. Four models' wrong bids go to the highest bid on the menu, the one most likely to win, about twice as often as chance; under pay-your-bid pricing this overpays the seller, while under eBay's own proxy rule it would not. Sending answers that disagree with the trace's own numbers to a calculator recovers 84--100\% of the gap on eBay and 82--100\% on held-out synthetic traces, calling it in 18--57\% of decisions.
