> Forcing 768 tokens raises $q$ by 0.11--0.18 (all intervals exclude zero after a Bonferroni correction), almost all of it above 256 tokens, and recovers 42--85\% of the calculator's lead (Table~\ref{tab:main}); budget-zero answers ($q=0.72$--0.77) are within 0.06 of random. Re-scoring the same choices under a lognormal fit to held-out prices gives 44--85\%, and under a win curve consistent with eBay's mechanism, where a new bidder must beat the highest of each auction's $n$ rival values (a lognormal value distribution fitted by maximum likelihood to closing prices as second-highest values), 43--87\%. The unforced run mostly measures more tokens: 46--87\% of its traces reach the 1{,}024 cap for five models, while Granite-8B always stops, by 669 tokens in 90\% of traces, and scores slightly better unforced (0.912) than forced to 768 (0.897). Dollar recovery is lower for five models because Cartier dominates the dollar gap (\$113--\$186 per decision at budget zero, \$2--\$15 elsewhere). Sampled traces vary little across five seeds (seed s.d. of $q$ 0.001--0.017); a five-sample majority vote at 768 tokens adds 0.005--0.040 for five times the tokens.
