< We compare each wrong answer with the non-optimal options on the same menu. DeepSeek-8B and the Qwen models take the highest bid, which is also the most likely to win, in 40--48\% of their wrong answers against 18--19\% by chance, about twice as often; these choices carry 63--78\% of the loss from missing a written best. Phi-mini takes the first-listed option in 43\% of wrong answers (chance 18\%) and almost never the last (1\%); Granite-8B takes the runner-up in 47\% (chance 20\%) and almost never the first-listed option (3\%). All six repeat their own budget-zero answer in 28--62\% of wrong answers (chance 17--18\%). Within a scenario the effect of the best option's own position is model-specific: with scenario fixed effects over the three option orders, moving it from first to last multiplies the odds of passing over it by 12 [7, 21] for Phi-mini (369 events), 3.4 [1.3, 8.8], 35 [6, 197] and 14 [3, 54] for Qwen3-4B, 8B and 14B (52--85 events) and 0.06 [0.03, 0.13] for Granite-8B (221), with no clear effect for DeepSeek-8B (0.67 [0.28, 1.6]). On the synthetic auctions Phi-mini (17 [6, 48]) and Granite-8B (0.09 [0.02, 0.39]) repeat the pattern; the others have too few events. The free-text answer names the calculator's bid in at most 25\% of passed-over traces, so this is not an artifact of the letter readout.
