< Among wrong answers, 81--91\% of those of DeepSeek-8B and the Qwen models bid above the calculator, against 55--61\% if they were drawn at random from the same menus; Phi-mini (60\% vs 51\%) and Granite-8B (51\% vs 46\%) are close to chance. Under the pay-your-bid objective, these wrong answers raise the chance of winning by 8--14 points but raise the expected payment from \$239--\$339 to \$459--\$717 per decision, so they buy probability at a loss of \$94--\$164 of expected profit each. The tilt toward high bids holds when the choices are re-scored: 47--65\% of wrong answers overbid under the lognormal curve and 73--89\% under shaded prices. Lost expected profit over all decisions is 2.0--5.2\% of the private value for Cartier, 0.3--3.8\% for Xbox and at most 0.8\% for Palm. The cost depends on the pricing rule. Under eBay's own rule, where the winner pays about the closing price, a higher bid is never worse: the same overbids gain \$39--\$121 against the calculator's choice, and five models' budget-zero answers already score above it (0.91--0.98 vs 0.89). The high-bid drift is a loss only where bidders pay their bid.
---
> Per 1{,}000 decisions at 768 tokens, against the calculator's choices, pay-your-bid pricing on the empirical curve moves \$18k--\$83k of expected revenue to sellers and costs bidders \$15k--\$36k of surplus, mostly on Cartier; on the order-statistic curve the figures are \$16k--\$62k and \$5k--\$14k. The tilt toward high bids is a fact about the menu, but whether it is overbidding depends on the curve: 81--91\% of the wrong answers of DeepSeek-8B and the Qwen models bid above the calculator on the empirical curve (55--61\% by chance), but only 38--57\% of all models' wrong answers bid above the optimum on the order-statistic curve. Under eBay's own rule, where the winner pays about the closing price, higher bids are never worse: the same choices raise both seller revenue (\$5k--\$26k) and bidder surplus (\$8k--\$21k for five models), and five models' budget-zero answers score above the calculator's choice (0.91--0.98 vs 0.89). The economic cost of the errors is therefore a property of pay-your-bid pricing.
